WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.560
So as we've seen Donald Trump second impeachment&nbsp;
trial get in day two and then now into day three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.400
there have been a lot of questions about&nbsp;
just what has Donald Trump actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.920
doing these last few days and even the&nbsp;
couple of weeks since he left office.&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.720
And there's a very interesting new article&nbsp;
in The Washington Post from Josh Dossi and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.440
Ashley Parker that tells the story of Donald&nbsp;
Trump at Mar a Lago in Palm Beach, Florida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.200
doing a lot of golfing, not surprisingly,&nbsp;
doing a lot of just calling old friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.360
trying to reconnect with people. He reportedly&nbsp;
continues to have many political aides on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.120
payroll just around him, sycophants of sorts of&nbsp;
different kinds. But this Washington Post article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.040
really tells the story of a former president and&nbsp;
former sort of big shot businessman, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.640
at least to some degree, whose world is really&nbsp;
crumbling around him, increasingly isolated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.640
from most of the of popular culture, half the&nbsp;
country, or more disgusted with him, unwilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:04.320
to patronize his businesses anymore. And while,&nbsp;
as Josh Dossey writes in The Washington Post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.800
Trump is confident that he is going to be&nbsp;
acquitted. He is increasingly disturbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.000
partially by the absolutely bizarre performance&nbsp;
of his attorney, Bruce Castor, on Monday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.520
the free association Rorschach test sort of thing&nbsp;
that took place, which we covered yesterday. Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:29.760
disappointed in Bruce Caster's performance. Donald&nbsp;
Trump also reportedly not pleased in general with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.840
how Republicans have been. Some Republicans have&nbsp;
been responding to the evidence presented so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.520
And Donald Trump is in this sort of bubble. And&nbsp;
we know that Donald Trump was in a bubble while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.560
was president of the United States. But he seems&nbsp;
to sort of further be protecting himself with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.920
bubble. And part of it, interestingly enough,&nbsp;
is coming from the fact that Donald Trump is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.560
banned from a lot of social media platforms where&nbsp;
Donald Trump not banned from Twitter right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.280
He would be not only tweeting, but also spending&nbsp;
more time on Twitter and maybe seeing that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.560
are even many Republicans disgusted with Donald&nbsp;
Trump's role in inciting the riots on January&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.000
6th. The fact that Donald Trump has been removed&nbsp;
from Twitter and a number of other platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.440
has placed him in what many people around him are&nbsp;
saying. It is a state of being adrift. No idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.360
what to do day to day, no idea of what comes next&nbsp;
and whether there is a future for Donald Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.560
political life and business life. And I think this&nbsp;
is another aspect that it just sort of remains to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:38.320
be seen like this. Donald Trump go back to doing&nbsp;
real estate deals at this point. The one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.960
that has been mentioned by a number of sources&nbsp;
that Dorsey and Parker cite in The Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:49.120
Post piece is that Donald Trump is calmer&nbsp;
than was expected. He was reportedly screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:56.320
about Bruce Caster's terrible performance. But&nbsp;
Trump seems relatively calm as he is adrift and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:03.840
unclear about what the future is of his business&nbsp;
and political life. But Donald Trump reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.640
furious with some Republicans, including&nbsp;
Congresswoman Liz Cheney, who did vote to impeach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.560
Donald Trump in the House of Representatives.&nbsp;
House Republican leader Kevin McCarthy, who said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.800
Trump does have some responsibility for the attack&nbsp;
on the Capitol, then ended up backtracking when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:30.880
those comments exploded. So the general sense&nbsp;
here is that this is a Trump surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.560
the most extreme sycophants, because who else&nbsp;
would be left at this point to even be around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.720
Donald Trump, given what's taken place off social&nbsp;
media, partially because he's been kicked off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.600
furious with the Republicans that he believes have&nbsp;
abandoned him and unclear as to what the future of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.640
his life even is at this point in time.
Now, one other interesting detail about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.920
Donald Trump during the impeachment&nbsp;
trial and in the days leading up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.480
early on, you'll remember that a bunch of Donald&nbsp;
Trump's lawyers for the second impeachment trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.520
quit. It was, I guess, a week ago Sunday,&nbsp;
not this past Sunday, but the previous one.&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.560
Part of what we believe led to that is that&nbsp;
Donald Trump was insisting to his first legal team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.280
that they defend Trump in the impeachment trial&nbsp;
by arguing that Trump's claims about a stolen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:31.120
election were were true. This is sort of like an&nbsp;
affirmative defense. If you're accused of killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.280
someone and you're being charged with murder,&nbsp;
one defense would be I didn't do it either.&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.640
I didn't kill anybody. An affirmative defense&nbsp;
would be I did kill them. But it's not murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.880
because it was self-defense. And what Donald Trump&nbsp;
wanted was the affirmative defense of, OK, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.720
I did tell people to go to the capital and they&nbsp;
were being inspired by me, but it was because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:02.000
I rightly identified that the election was stolen&nbsp;
from me. Trump's initial. Defense team reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.360
thought that that was an absolutely terrible idea,&nbsp;
knowing Donald Trump's instinct for self sabotage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.200
and inflicting, you know, unforced, unforced&nbsp;
problems upon himself. They didn't go with that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.160
And Donald Trump ultimately getting new lawyers.&nbsp;
But that is not the case that is being made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.800
the case that we we have already seen previewed&nbsp;
by Donald Trump's attorneys, Bruce Castor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.640
David Schoen, and which we are going to continue&nbsp;
to see today. And as the the defense mounts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:35.280
simply that Donald Trump didn't incite the riot&nbsp;
period and that another source of isolation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.360
fury and confusion from Donald Trump. So I don't&nbsp;
know what the future of Donald Trump's life is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.560
What I hope happens is that the country can move&nbsp;
on from Trump. But at the same time and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.320
going to talk about this on the Friday show,&nbsp;
I do actually hope for the sake of the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:56.800
that Donald Trump tries to start a third party&nbsp;
because so many Republicans have said in surveys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.200
that they'd be willing to abandon the Republican&nbsp;
Party and follow Trump Trump starting a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.400
party. It could be exactly what the left needs&nbsp;
to really make some progress over the next two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.640
four, six, even eight years. So we'll be&nbsp;
talking more about that on tomorrow's program.

